WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.238
DHCP failover, and IPv6.

2
00:00:03.238 --> 00:00:06.500
The rules, from Infoblox's failover page.

3
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:08.770
Peers are two NIOS servers,

4
00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:13.825
or NIOS and an ISC-compliant
server; at least one must be NIOS.

5
00:00:13.825 --> 00:00:16.638
A mixed pair of Windows
and NIOS isn't offered,

6
00:00:16.638 --> 00:00:20.667
and that's why the Federal
Modernization Workbook's Book 08 says

7
00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:24.925
to move both partners of a Windows
failover relationship together.

8
00:00:24.925 --> 00:00:27.963
Peers talk on TCP 647 by default,

9
00:00:27.963 --> 00:00:34.975
or 519 on Grids upgraded from older
versions, and on 7911 for partner down.

10
00:00:34.975 --> 00:00:39.537
The defaults are a fifty-fifty load balance
and a maximum client lead time of one

11
00:00:39.537 --> 00:00:44.237
hour, and Infoblox says not to change them
unless you understand the consequences.

12
00:00:44.237 --> 00:00:48.078
Each pair of servers can be
in only one association,

13
00:00:48.078 --> 00:00:51.187
covering IPv4 ranges in one network view.

14
00:00:51.187 --> 00:00:54.601
And configure everything
before you enable DHCP.

15
00:00:54.601 --> 00:00:59.825
One correction to a common assumption:
the Grid Master plays no part in failover.

16
00:00:59.825 --> 00:01:01.375
The peers talk directly.

17
00:01:01.375 --> 00:01:04.577
On Azure, failover
doesn't work behind NAT,

18
00:01:04.577 --> 00:01:09.850
and Universal DDI's portal can't
manage NIOS failover associations.

19
00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:11.737
Then IPv6.

20
00:01:11.737 --> 00:01:15.062
An IPv6 range is served
by a single Grid member;

21
00:01:15.062 --> 00:01:17.862
there's no failover association for it.

22
00:01:17.862 --> 00:01:22.927
So redundancy comes from making that
member an HA pair, served through its

23
00:01:22.927 --> 00:01:28.325
virtual address, or, by my inference, from
non-overlapping ranges on two members.

24
00:01:28.325 --> 00:01:33.475
That isn't a regression: Microsoft's
DHCPv6 has no failover either.

25
00:01:33.475 --> 00:01:40.925
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x Configuring
DHCP failover associations (rules, ports,

26
00:01:40.925 --> 00:01:47.596
load balance, MCLT); IPv6 range wizard;
About bloxHA; DHCPv6 lease affinity;

27
00:01:47.596 --> 00:01:53.489
vNIOS for Azure limitations;
earlier platform brief (Universal

28
00:01:53.489 --> 00:01:58.990
DDI hybrid limits); Microsoft
Learn DHCP failover overview.
